{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0011", "date": "2026-09-11", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "fixup3_landing", "element": "model", "observation": "FIXUP-3 p01's third execution, the first substantive author on claude-opus-5 under OW-13, landed clean: 5 rows replaced, 21 of 21 orders executed, no form defect, local tiling PASS, normalizer defects 0. By its own record, the installed suite over private copies read HARD GREEN, and register flags on its rows went from 12 to 0. It also fixed six defects inside its rows that no order named, and disclosed them for review; flagged a count claim it left open; and found a brief wording bug in the tiling self-check. It had one E-19 (b) deviation, a setup script written at the scratchpad root and then moved into its private directory, self-reported and verified contained. The desktop app's exit interrupted it; after a resume message it continued from its transcript. For comparison, the five Sonnet parts that landed carried three E-19 lapses, one of them undisclosed, and one skipped installed suite.", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/fixup3/ezek_author_fixup_attempt_receipts.jsonl ezek_author_fixup3_p01_a1#e3", "sp_durable/Ezek/freeze/CYCLE_STATE.md 'FIXUP-3 LANDED, BATCH 5: p01' and the p01 #e3 stop/resume entry", "M8_fable/orchestration_learning_log.v1.jsonl L-0006, L-0010"], "metric": {"model_ordered": "claude-opus-5", "rows_replaced": 5, "orders_executed": 21, "orders_total": 21, "form_defects": 0, "tokens_reported_resumed_segment": 318739, "tool_calls_in_transcript": 37, "tool_calls_before_stop": 23, "e19_deviations": 1, "e19_deviations_self_reported": 1, "unordered_edits_disclosed": 6}, "verdict": "open", "recommendation": "Close with S4's verdict on these rows, including the six unordered edits, and compare findings per replaced row between the Opus-authored part and the Sonnet-authored parts before routing Daniel's authoring. Resume-by-message after an interruption kept all of the execution's work; its usage record covers only the resumed segment, so receipts should mark that gap.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0012", "date": "2026-09-12", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "fixup3_apply", "element": "wording", "observation": "A brief that offers example sentence shapes as literal wording converges across authors. The FIXUP-3 brief's mark-disclosure shape A ends 'at the seam this unit opens on'. That tail already stood in 6 rows from FIXUP-2, so only three rows of headroom remained under the ngram7 gate of 10. p03's author used it in four new disclosure sentences, and the applied corpus failed the HARD ngram7 gate on three sub-grams of that one phrase, which also put CWO-EZ-07 at NOT_COVERED. The same author had skipped the installed validator suite, which would have caught it before delivery. Shapes B and C from the same brief now stand in 4 and 3 rows, all from that author.", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/repair/suite_v6_6c843b14/rows.jsonl.validator_report.json ngram7", "sp_durable/Ezek/freeze/CYCLE_STATE.md 'FIXUP-3 APPLIED (rows_v6_fixup3, RUN 1)'", "sp_durable/Ezek/FIXUP3_BRIEF.md FORMULATIONS", "M8_fable/orchestration_learning_log.v1.jsonl L-0006"], "metric": {"gate": 10, "offending_grams": 3, "rows_per_gram": 10, "rows_from_this_wave": 4, "rows_from_earlier_waves": 6, "shape_b_rows": 4, "shape_c_rows": 3}, "verdict": "failed", "recommendation": "A brief builder counts every example shape's seven-word runs over the corpus it will be written into, and refuses any shape whose remaining headroom is smaller than the wave's part count; or it states the ruled form without literal wording. A relaunch message states the measured count of every shape. An author that skips the installed suite is recorded at landing and read by the distinct checker.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0013", "date": "2026-09-12", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "fixup3_apply", "element": "tool", "observation": "Tool digest pins break when a tool the campaign later repairs is pinned by a single install receipt. The CWO-EZ-14..18 coverage tool checked every suite member against the TOOLFIX-2 install receipt and aborted over rows_v6, because TOOLFIX-5 had replaced check_register.py and check_web_quotes.py. A sibling tool for CWO-EZ-19..21 accepted only stages v4 and v5 and could not report over v6 at all. Both were plumbing, not judgment, and both were installed with a preimage, a diff for the distinct checker and a receipt; the first reproduced all three v5 reports byte for byte, and the second left 42 earlier coverage reports unchanged.", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/campaign/receipts/ezek_tools_install_fixup3_plumbing.json", "sp_durable/campaign/receipts/ezek_tools_install_fixup3_plumbing_coverage14.json", "sp_durable/Ezek/proposals/fixup3_plumbing/", "sp_durable/Ezek/freeze/CYCLE_STATE.md 'FIXUP-3 APPLIED (rows_v6_fixup3, RUN 1)'"], "metric": {"tools_patched": 2, "regression_v5_reports_reproduced": 3, "earlier_coverage_reports_unchanged": 42}, "verdict": "mixed", "recommendation": "A tool that verifies another tool's digest names the receipt per member, not one receipt for all, so a later repair of one member does not stop an unrelated sweep. Stage every such change with a preimage, a diff the distinct checker reads, and a regression that reproduces existing outputs byte for byte.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0014", "date": "2026-09-13", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "fixup3_apply", "element": "control", "observation": "The control recorded for the ngram7 failure is now an installed campaign tool, campaign/_brief_shape_headroom.py: it measures a brief's example wording against the corpus that wave will be written into and refuses a shape whose headroom is smaller than the wave's part count (gate - 1 - rows_already_carrying_the_gram >= parts). It imports the book's installed ngram7 and uses that tool's own prose walk, stripping and WEB exclusion rather than a copy, because a private tokenizer would drift from the gate it predicts; it realizes a shape's placeholders to a concrete key first, since the installed tool strips real citations as apparatus and would otherwise measure grams no author can write. Its selftest is the historical case: over the pre-wave chain head with FIXUP-3's own five shapes and six parts it refuses shape A at 6 rows, headroom 3, naming exactly the six rows the record names, and over the applied corpus the same run stands in 10. The install refuses if that selftest misses. Running it also caught an orchestrator error: the CYCLE_STATE entry stated shapes B and C stood in '4 and 3 rows, all p03's', written from reading the wave's rows; measured, each stands in 4, and one of C's predates the wave. Both lines were corrected in place before any digest was pinned.", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/campaign/_brief_shape_headroom.py", "sp_durable/campaign/receipts/ezek_tools_install_brief_shape_headroom.json", "sp_durable/orchestrator_scratch/ezek_post_s2/headroom_v5_prewave.json", "sp_durable/Ezek/freeze/CYCLE_STATE.md 'CORRECTED a second time'", "M8_fable/orchestration_learning_log.v1.jsonl L-0012"], "metric": {"selftest_checks": 6, "prewave_shape_a_rows": 6, "prewave_headroom": 3, "parts": 6, "applied_shape_a_rows": 10, "shapes_refused_on_applied_corpus": 3, "record_figures_corrected": 2}, "verdict": "worked", "recommendation": "A brief builder runs this tool over its own example wording before the brief is written, and a wave does not launch on a brief whose shapes it refuses. More generally: a figure that sounds measured is measured, and a record states the tool that measured it - the orchestrator's own summaries are subject to the same rule it imposes on authors.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0015", "date": "2026-09-13", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "fixup3_relaunch", "element": "model", "observation": "The first Opus author execution ordered under OW-13 to repair another model's work cured the failure cleanly. p03's #e1 ran on claude-sonnet-5, took the brief's example wording into four disclosure sentences and skipped the installed validator suite, and the applied corpus failed the HARD ngram7 gate. #e2 ran the same orders on claude-opus-5: it imported the gate's own tool to replicate its tokenization, measured the background gram counts over the applied corpus, wrote all twelve disclosures from its own rows' bytes reusing none of the brief's five shapes and neither crowded background shape, and ran the installed suite over a private substituted copy before delivering (ngram7 0 offending, highest combined reuse 9). It also removed 8 CWO-EZ-22 arm hits, and disclosed three edits beyond the fields its orders name - two byte-false paseq statements and one overclaim standing in a third field of a row it emits whole - rather than either silently fixing or silently leaving them. It cost 314,747 tokens against #e1's 213,432, a 47% premium for a re-execution that landed with no form defect and took the applied suite from RED to GREEN.", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/fixup3/ezek_author_fixup_attempt_receipts.jsonl ezek_author_fixup3_p03_a1#e2", "sp_durable/Ezek/repair/suite_v6_6c843b14/rows.jsonl.validator_report.json", "sp_durable/transcripts/6116e665-408b-4874-9bf0-881eb9c464f5/agent-a7e45cd1351d93560.jsonl", "M8_fable/orchestration_learning_log.v1.jsonl L-0012"], "metric": {"tokens_e1_sonnet": 213432, "tokens_e2_opus": 314747, "tool_uses": 24, "rows_replaced": 13, "ngram7_offending_before": 3, "ngram7_offending_after": 0, "cwo22_arm_hits_before": 8, "cwo22_arm_hits_after": 0, "form_defects": 0, "edits_beyond_orders_disclosed": 3}, "verdict": "worked", "recommendation": "Substantive repair of another model's rows goes to Opus under OW-13, and the premium is worth paying where a HARD gate is at stake. An author brief should require what this execution did unprompted: replicate the gate's tokenization from the installed tool, measure against the corpus, and run the installed suite before delivering.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0016", "date": "2026-09-13", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "fixup3_relaunch", "element": "wording", "observation": "A relaunch message that hands the author the measurement rather than an instruction to vary produced a targeted re-execution. It quoted the controlling ruling verbatim, named the three offending grams and the four rows of its own that carried them, pinned the applied corpus by digest as the corpus to measure against, stated the measured row count of every example shape in the original brief, and offered the author's own #e1 deliverable as a starting point. The author then avoided all five brief shapes and both crowded background shapes, and reported back the two gram families still sitting at 9 rows against a gate of 10 - one of them seven of its own pre-existing rows it had no order to rewrite - which is the kind of residual risk a 'vary your wording' instruction never surfaces.", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/orchestrator_scratch/ezek_post_s2/_fixup3_relaunch_message.v3.py", "sp_durable/Ezek/fixup3/ezek_author_fixup_attempt_receipts.jsonl ezek_author_fixup3_p03_a1#e2 unresolved_uncertainty", "sp_durable/Ezek/freeze/CYCLE_STATE.md 'FIXUP-3 APPLIED (rows_v6_fixup3, RUN 1)'", "M8_fable/orchestration_learning_log.v1.jsonl L-0014"], "metric": {"grams_named": 3, "own_rows_named": 4, "shapes_with_measured_counts": 5, "residual_gram_families_at_9_rows_reported_back": 2, "rows_in_the_largest_residual_family": 9}, "verdict": "worked", "recommendation": "Every relaunch after a gate failure names the failing artifact by digest, the exact offending strings, the author's own rows that carry them, and the measured headroom of any wording the earlier brief supplied. Residual counts an author reports back are carried into the next brief and to the distinct checker, because they are the cheapest early warning of the next gate failure.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0017", "date": "2026-09-13", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "open_items_transcripts", "element": "control", "observation": "A ruling can order a lookup along a route that does not exist, and the campaign's own counts can be wrong in the safe-sounding direction. #e10 OPEN-ITEMS (a) ordered the Jeremiah and Lamentations transcripts found 'by exact path from each book's transcript map'. Neither book has a transcript map - Ezekiel's is the only one on disk - and campaign/capture_index.v1.jsonl carries an agent_id field for all 135 Jer and Lam rows whose every value is the literal string 'UNAVAILABLE'. The route that did work was the preserved store's own _index.json, whose task-file name stems ARE runtime agent ids, resolved by exact path into <session>/subagents/agent-<id>.jsonl per OW-11-m. Against a census recording Jeremiah at 0 transcripts retained and Lamentations at 5 retained with 32 mapped-but-zero-bytes, the session actually held 115 transcripts and 115 meta files totalling 90,640,229 bytes with no zero-byte file. All 230 were preserved with digests, 0 conflicts, nothing earlier rewritten. 170 of the 230 are bound to no attempt by any record now on disk, so most of that evidence cannot be attributed to a book at all. The earlier manifest's paths pointed at the session TASKS tree, whose .output files hold 0 bytes - it was built before OW-11-m established that the live transcripts sit in the SUBAGENTS tree.", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/campaign/finding_jer_lam_transcripts_present_after_census.v1.json", "sp_durable/campaign/transcript_coverage_census.v1.json", "sp_durable/campaign/transcript_manifest.v1.json", "sp_durable/transcripts/dce0b6e2-5586-41d9-8b2f-26f22fb19fa5/_index.v2.json", "sp_durable/Ezek/freeze/CYCLE_STATE.md '#e10 OPEN-ITEMS (a) DONE'"], "metric": {"files_preserved": 230, "transcripts": 115, "meta_files": 115, "bytes": 90640229, "zero_byte_files": 0, "bound_to_an_attempt": 60, "unbound": 170, "conflicts": 0, "census_jer_retained": 0, "census_lam_retained": 5, "census_lam_mapped_but_zero": 32, "capture_index_rows_with_unavailable_agent_id": 135}, "verdict": "mixed", "recommendation": "Every book writes its transcript map WHILE its agents run, in the Ezekiel form: an agent id not recorded at launch cannot be recovered from any later artifact, and 'UNAVAILABLE' in a capture index is a permanent loss of the route, not a formatting detail. A retention census reads the tree the transcripts are actually in and states which tree it read. A count that a later check contradicts is corrected by a new finding standing beside it under its digest, never by editing the earlier record. Preserve on discovery: the files were one runtime cleanup away from being unrecoverable.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0018", "date": "2026-09-13", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "s4_in_flight", "element": "control", "observation": "A brief's deliverable schema and the landing tool's validator are two statements of the same contract, and nobody checked them against each other. S4's brief was built from S3's template and launched with four keys the installed validator refuses: cwo23_pairs where validate_s4 requires cwo_ez_23_pairs; mark_sample.rows where it requires mark_disclosures.p03 (exactly 12) and mark_disclosures.others (at least 10); a flat cwo_recomputed where it requires cwo_recomputed.residuals carrying all nineteen CWO ids as integers, CWO-EZ-03 included though no coverage report for it exists on disk; and p04_008_readback.verdict as accept|defect where it requires cured|not_cured. The mismatch was found while the execution was still running, by reading validate_s4 to prepare the cure-claims script, not by any control. The correction went to the agent by message, since the brief is pinned by its own in-flight execution and editing it would break the pin check the agent already verified. Had it landed unnoticed, a scarce claude-fable-5-1 execution would have produced a deliverable the landing tool refuses, at a moment when the owner's Fable quota was near exhaustion. A sibling audit clears the systemic worry: S3's brief and validate_s3 agree, because validate_s3 genuinely requires mark_sample.rows with at least ten entries. The defect arose from copying S3's schema into S4, whose validator states a stricter contract - the template was right for its own job and wrong for the next one.", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/_land_rulings_and_t1.py validate_s4", "sp_durable/orchestrator_scratch/ezek_post_s2/_s4_brief.py SCHEMA", "sp_durable/Ezek/FIXUP3_WAVE_REVIEW_BRIEF_S4.md", "sp_durable/Ezek/freeze/CYCLE_STATE.md 'S4 LAUNCHED ON claude-fable-5-1'"], "metric": {"keys_mismatched": 4, "validator_required_cwo_residual_keys": 19, "coverage_reports_on_disk": 18, "found_while_running": true, "brief_bytes_changed": 0, "corrections_sent_by_message": 1}, "verdict": "failed", "recommendation": "A brief builder that writes a deliverable schema for a job with an installed landing validator checks the two against each other BEFORE the launch, and refuses on any disagreement - the validator is the contract, the brief is only its restatement. Where a validator demands a value no artifact on disk can supply (CWO-EZ-03 has no coverage report), the brief says so in words and tells the agent to measure it. A brief already pinned by a running execution is never edited: the correction goes to the agent by message and into the builder for the next run.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0019", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "pre_primaries_budget", "element": "tool", "observation": "A script a gate names by name carried a superseded constant, and the gate would have shown the owner the wrong number. GATE-E10 says budget_pre_primaries.py is re-run at the measured census after S4 lands and the owner is checked in with if the projected high crosses the ceiling. That script still hardcoded CEILING = 21,500,000, the figure the owner's third answer of 2026-09-11 replaced with 49,604,821, and still read the stale corpus rows_v5_fixup2.jsonl rather than the chain head. Run today it reports headroom -1,433,978, which reads as a breach already incurred; against the ceiling that actually binds, the same census of 22,933,978 leaves 26,670,843. The projection itself was sound: low 17,972,936 and high 28,471,747, so the projected total at close is 40,906,914 to 51,405,725 and the high does cross 49,604,821 - the check-in GATE-E10 requires is genuinely required, but for a reason two orders of magnitude away from what the stale run would have said. v1 was not edited: v2 stands beside it with a changed_since_v1 block, because v1's 2026-09-11 output is already recorded and comparable.", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/orchestrator_scratch/ezek_post_s2/budget_pre_primaries.py", "sp_durable/orchestrator_scratch/ezek_post_s2/budget_pre_primaries.v2.py", "sp_durable/orchestrator_scratch/ezek_post_s2/budget_pre_primaries.v2.json", "M8_fable/ERROR_PATTERN_LEDGER.v1.md OW-11-n"], "metric": {"stale_ceiling": 21500000, "binding_ceiling": 49604821, "census": 22933978, "headroom_reported_by_v1": -1433978, "headroom_against_the_binding_ceiling": 26670843, "projection_low": 17972936, "projection_high": 28471747, "projected_total_high_at_close": 51405725, "check_in_required": true}, "verdict": "failed", "recommendation": "A number an owner can change - a ceiling, a budget, a cap - is never a hardcoded constant in a script a gate names. It is read from the carrier the owner's answer updates, or the script refuses. When control (u) records an owner answer in every carrier, the scripts that consume it are part of that sweep: the ledger, the gate, CYCLE_STATE and the checker clause were all updated on 2026-09-11 while this script was not. Version rather than edit a script whose earlier output is already on the record, so the two runs stay comparable.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0020", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "s4_stopped", "element": "control", "observation": "A runtime failure notification shows the agent's FIRST words, not its last, and reading it as a status report nearly cost a completed review. S4 was killed by an API rate_limit (HTTP 429, the account's claude-fable-5-1 credits exhausted) at the moment it emitted its final message. The notification's result field carried one sentence - 'I'll start with the governance files my policy requires' - so the execution looked like a dead start. On that reading the orchestrator told the owner there was no deliverable, and the owner chose to re-run the whole distinct check on another model. In fact the execution had made 95 tool calls and written a complete 108,580-byte deliverable: fit_with_changes, bound to the applied corpus's exact digest, all 45 replaced rows, 10 question dispositions, 12 p03 and 14 other mark disclosures, 9 of 9 re-splice runs, all 19 CWO residuals zero, readiness fit=true in the T6-02 ACCEPT form with per-S1, per-S2 and per-S3 statuses, and 12 findings, none above minor. It had also applied the schema correction sent to it mid-flight. What the failure actually destroyed was narrow: the self-authored OW-8 record, which exists neither in the notification nor anywhere in the 1.8 MB preserved transcript, whose stream ends at the error's request_id. The mistake was caught by a guard rather than by judgment - the record script refused because it found a deliverable at the exact path it was asserting did not exist - and the owner was corrected before anything was spent.", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/pending_s4/NOTE.json", "sp_durable/Ezek/ezek_rulings_attempt_receipts.jsonl ezek_fixup3_wave_review_s4_a1#e1", "sp_durable/transcripts/6116e665-408b-4874-9bf0-881eb9c464f5/agent-a1a93e16761b00381.jsonl", "sp_durable/Ezek/evidence_notes/ezek_fixup3_wave_review_s4_a1%23e1.json"], "metric": {"tool_calls": 95, "deliverable_bytes": 108580, "findings": 12, "max_severity": "minor", "cwo_residuals_all_zero": true, "replaced_rows_reviewed": 45, "final_record_recovered": false, "transcript_bytes": 1839554, "wasted_executions": 0}, "verdict": "mixed", "recommendation": "On ANY failed or stopped notification, check the deliverable path by exact path before describing the execution, and say what the notification can and cannot tell you: it carries the agent's opening text, the error and sometimes no usage at all. Preserve a completed deliverable out of the session scratchpad immediately and record it as HELD, never as landed. Where the runtime kills an execution at emission, the fix is to resume that same execution id for its record once quota returns - not to re-run the work, and not to hand-write the record the capture law requires the agent to author.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0021", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "s4_landed", "element": "control", "observation": "Recording a stopped execution as a receipt made the ledger contradict itself once that execution later landed. When the runtime killed S4 at its final message, the orchestrator appended a receipt with outcome STOPPED_AT_FINAL_MESSAGE and no output file. The owner chose to resume the same execution id for its missing record; it emitted the record without redoing any work (tool_uses 0), and the landing tool landed the deliverable with parity, form_defects 0 and the capture index GREEN - but, being correctly idempotent by execution id, it did not append a second receipt. The ledger was left saying the execution produced nothing while the artifact stood landed beside it, and the cure-claims script, which requires a single receipt reading LANDED with a matching digest, would have refused. Receipts are append-only, so the fix was an amendment row naming the amended line, its outcome before and after, the landed digest and the resumed-segment usage, with the original bytes untouched.", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/ezek_rulings_attempt_receipts.jsonl ezek_fixup3_wave_review_s4_a1#e1 and its amendment", "sp_durable/Ezek/ezek_fixup3_wave_review_S4.json", "sp_durable/Ezek/pending_s4/NOTE.json", "M8_fable/orchestration_learning_log.v1.jsonl L-0020"], "metric": {"receipts_before": 21, "amendment_rows": 1, "resumed_segment_tokens": 646412, "resumed_segment_tool_uses": 0, "review_tool_calls_never_costed": 95, "form_defects_at_landing": 0}, "verdict": "mixed", "recommendation": "An execution that is stopped but resumable is recorded as an evidence note and a CYCLE_STATE entry, NOT as a receipt with a terminal outcome - a receipt asserts how the execution ended, and a resumable one has not ended. Where a terminal receipt has already been written and the execution later lands, append an amendment that names the line it amends and binds the landed digest, and make every consumer resolve amendments before reading an outcome. Record plainly that a resumed segment's token count is not the cost of the work done before the stop.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0022", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "s4_landed", "element": "control", "observation": "The transcript audit caught an undisclosed E-19 lapse in the distinct CHECKER itself, which is the second instance of the p08 class and the first in a checking lane. S4's final record states it 'ran no listing, no glob, no recursive search, no directory existence check ... my own scratch included'. Its transcript shows 95 tool calls, of which six were Grep: five searched a single named file by exact path, which is a content read and not a listing, but the sixth ran with glob '*.json' against a DIRECTORY - its own private copies directory at scratchpad/ezek_s4_k4v9q2xw/copies/Ezek - searching for CWO-EZ-03. The brief's exact-path law bars a glob or recursive search anywhere, its own scratch included, so the claim is contradicted by the record of what it did. The lapse is contained: no worktree path was involved, all 20 Writes and both Edits went to its own scratchpad, none of its 18 shell commands carried a listing or existence idiom, and no row or verdict depends on the call. Everything else it disclosed checked out, including four preimage paths taken from pinned inputs' own fields and 23 files hashed but not read.", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/transcripts/6116e665-408b-4874-9bf0-881eb9c464f5/agent-a1a93e16761b00381.jsonl", "sp_durable/Ezek/ezek_fixup3_wave_review_S4.json e19_selfreport", "sp_durable/Ezek/freeze/CYCLE_STATE.md 'S4 LANDED'", "M8_fable/orchestration_learning_log.v1.jsonl L-0010"], "metric": {"tool_calls": 95, "reads": 49, "writes": 20, "edits": 2, "shell": 18, "greps": 6, "greps_on_an_exact_file": 5, "glob_searches_over_a_directory": 1, "writes_under_the_worktree": 0, "shell_listing_idioms": 0, "undisclosed_lapses": 1}, "verdict": "mixed", "recommendation": "Audit the checker's transcript exactly as an author's: a self-report is evidence of intent, never of conduct, and the lane that checks others is not exempt. A brief that bars globs should say in words that a content search with a glob or a directory path is one, since the lapse here looks like a read to the agent that made it. Keep classifying by the tool's own arguments - path plus glob - rather than by the tool's name.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0023", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "pre_primaries_q8_dry_run", "element": "control", "observation": "A GATE-BEARING COUNT WAS MEASURED ON A ROUTE ITS PRODUCER HAD STOPPED WRITING. #e4 ruling (10) requires a Q8 DRY RUN before the real pre_primaries build. The dry run printed census Ezek transcript_retained 5 against a record stating all Ezekiel map transcripts are preserved. Measuring both routes rather than reconciling the prose: _transcript_coverage_census.py reads retention ONLY from **/transcript_manifest.v*.json, and Ezek/transcript_manifest.v1.json was built against the session TASKS route, whose .output files OW-11-m established hold 0 bytes. The preserver written under OW-11-m writes transcripts/_subagents_index.<book>.v1.json instead, and it records 77 of 77 agents found, 0 missing. Measured by exact path: 77 executions preserved, 74,247,663 bytes, covering 49 distinct attempt_ids. The census sees 5. FORTY-EIGHT of 66 attempts are readable on disk and invisible to the count the close quotes. A second defect of the same class sits in the Q8 tool itself: its behaviour_class_observed_now column reads TASKS/<agent_id>.output under ONE HARDCODED session root, so it classed the 77 preserved transcripts as B 35, unknown 42, A 3.", "evidence_refs": ["campaign/finding_ezek_transcript_manifest_stale_census_blind.v1.json", "Ezek/transcript_manifest.v2.CANDIDATE.json", "transcripts/_subagents_index.ezek.v1.json", "Ezek/transcript_manifest.v1.json", "campaign/_transcript_coverage_census.py", "Ezek/_coverage_statement_ezek.py"], "metric": {"attempts": 66, "retained_by_the_census_route": 5, "retained_on_disk_by_exact_path": 49, "invisible_to_the_census": 48, "executions_preserved": 77, "bytes_preserved": 74247663, "shipped_impact": 0}, "verdict": "open", "recommendation": "TWO WRITERS AND ONE CONSUMER MUST BE CONNECTED BY A CONTROL, NOT BY MEMORY. OW-11-m installed a new preservation route and its per-book index and rebuilt nothing downstream. OW-11-n control (u) sweeps every carrier when an OWNER ANSWER changes a number, but a ROUTE change is not an owner answer, so no control required the sweep - which is the gap. Extend control (u) to cover a change of ROUTE as well as a change of NUMBER: when a producer moves where it writes, the same step names every consumer that reads the old place. Until then, the dry run is what caught this, so keep the dry-run-before-real-build rule wherever a statement quotes a measured number. Routed to #e11 as a ruling, not plumbing: the census figure is close-gate item 14's and the OW-6 stage-1 slice denominator follows from it.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0024", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "pre_primaries_gate", "element": "tool", "observation": "A SCRIPT'S CODE WAS CURRENT AND ITS OUTPUT FILE WAS STALE, AND THE GATE READ THE FILE. budget_pre_primaries.v2.py was patched on 2026-09-14 to read the ceiling from campaign/budget_ceilings.v1.json (OW-15). budget_pre_primaries.v2.json BESIDE IT still held the 2026-09-11 run: ceiling 49,604,821, census 22,933,978, crosses_the_ceiling.high true, owner_check_in_required true. The pre-primaries gate check read the artifact and reported the clause OPEN. Re-running the script wrote ceiling 55,000,000, census 23,580,390, crosses false/false, and the clause then held. Nothing was wrong with the fix; the fix simply never re-ran the thing it fixed.", "evidence_refs": ["orchestrator_scratch/ezek_post_s2/budget_pre_primaries.v2.py", "orchestrator_scratch/ezek_post_s2/budget_pre_primaries.v2.json", "campaign/budget_ceilings.v1.json", "ezek_primaries/gate_e11_primaries_check.json (orchestrator scratch)"], "metric": {"stale_ceiling": 49604821, "current_ceiling": 55000000, "stale_census": 22933978, "current_census": 23580390, "clauses_wrongly_open": 1}, "verdict": "mixed", "recommendation": "THIS IS L-0019 ONE STEP DOWNSTREAM, AND IT ARGUES FOR THE GATE'S DESIGN RATHER THAN AGAINST IT. L-0019 was a script carrying a superseded constant; this is a script's OUTPUT carrying one. A gate that reads artifacts caught it; a gate that remembered what the script does would not have. Keep gates reading artifacts. Add to a tool patch's definition of done: RE-RUN THE TOOL AND REWRITE ITS OUTPUT, and record the new digest in the install receipt - a patch that leaves a stale output beside a fixed script is half-installed.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0025", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "pre_primaries_ow12_tool", "element": "tool", "observation": "BUILDING THE OW-12 STATUS TOOL SURFACED TWO DEFECTS IN ITS OWN FIRST DRAFT, BOTH CAUGHT BY MEASURING RATHER THAN ASSERTING. (1) Bucketing receipts by attempt_id prefix dropped S4's 646,412 tokens from Ezekiel, because an m8_attempt_receipt_amendment.v1 record carries amends_execution_id and its own book field but NO attempt_id. Caught by reconciling the tool's Ezekiel total against the census: 22,933,978 against 23,580,390. (2) A hand-written caveat asserted that 32 of the 40 books left are shorter than Lamentations; measured, it is 26, and the long-book list written beside it was also wrong. Both the count and the list are now COMPUTED by the tool. The tool also refuses three things the directive's wording invites: a token figure for the 23 books that closed before per-attempt capture, a per-book total presented as complete when only some executions carry usage (Lamentations: 31 of 61), and a tokens-per-active-day figure offered as a planning rate when recorded_at is when the RECEIPT was written, not when the work ran.", "evidence_refs": ["campaign/_book_close_status_ow12.py", "campaign/receipts/ezek_tools_install_ow12_status_tool.json", "Ezek/ezek_rulings_attempt_receipts.jsonl (the amendment line)"], "metric": {"tokens_dropped_by_the_first_bucketing": 646412, "asserted_count": 32, "measured_count": 26, "books_not_measurable_from_receipts": 23, "lam_usage_coverage": "31 of 61 executions"}, "verdict": "worked", "recommendation": "A REPORTING TOOL SHOULD COMPUTE ITS OWN CAVEATS. Every sentence in a status update that contains a number about the corpus should be generated from the corpus, including the sentences that explain the limitations - those are the ones nobody re-checks. This is the standing rule proposed by finding_correction_text_carries_its_own_defects.v1 applied to a status report: treat explanatory text as new unverified text and sweep it. Also: reconcile any new aggregate against an existing measured one before trusting it; the census is what caught the dropped amendment.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0026", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "e11_launch", "element": "control", "observation": "THE CHAINED GATE WAS COLLECTED AND MEASURED RATHER THAN READ FORWARD. conditions_before_primaries is a chain: #e10 says 'as #e9', #e9 says 'as #e8', back to #e3, across 8 executions and 50 clauses. A script collected every clause from the rulings files and decided the mechanically decidable ones from the bytes, then NAMED the rest as the controlling agent's - lane models, the gate verdict, S4's routing, the transcript route, the retained lows. Every mechanical clause held except the one deliberately left open. Two of the three initial FAILs were the checker's own harness, not the artifacts: gate_e10_s4_check.py prints a human table and emits JSON only with --json, and the budget artifact was stale (L-0024). Both were diagnosed before anything was reported as a defect of the lane.", "evidence_refs": ["ezek_primaries/gate_e11_primaries_check.py (orchestrator scratch)", "ezek_primaries/primaries_conditions.json (orchestrator scratch)", "Ezek/ezek_controlling_agent_queue_e11.v1.json", "campaign/receipts/ezek_tools_install_rulings_e11_landing_job.json"], "metric": {"executions_in_the_chain": 8, "clauses_collected": 50, "mechanical_clauses_measured": 8, "clauses_holding": 7, "clauses_open_by_design": 1, "harness_faults_misread_as_defects": 2}, "verdict": "worked", "recommendation": "WHEN A GATE IS A CHAIN, COLLECT IT INTO ONE ARTIFACT BEFORE RULING ON IT, and have the collector mark each clause mechanical or judgment. It stops the orchestrator quietly deciding a judgment clause by measuring something adjacent, and it gives the controlling agent one page instead of eight files. Also: when a checker reports a FAIL, diagnose the checker before reporting the lane - two of three here were the harness. The new rulings_e11 landing validator adds the first OW-13 arm in the campaign: a rulings deliverable is refused unless lane_models names a model AND a reason for every lane it authorises.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0027", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "e11_landed", "element": "control", "observation": "A SECOND SESSION RESUMING THE SAME CAMPAIGN WAS SURFACED BY THE IN-FLIGHT PIN GUARD, AND ITS ONE MEASUREMENT CHANGED A RULING. Two sessions resumed from the same RESUME_PROMPT_CURRENT.md against one registry-protected worktree. The second hit the pin on Ezek/ezek_controlling_agent_queue_e11.v1.json when its own builder ran the guard, disclosed itself by message rather than writing, and reported that a THIRD 7-gram family stands at headroom 0 where S4-11 names two. It was right. The orchestrator had carried S4's count into the #e11 queue without re-deriving it from the tool - the undercount was the orchestrator's, not the checker's. The pin guard was written to stop a record being written under a running execution; it detected a whole parallel session.", "evidence_refs": ["campaign/_inflight_pin_guard.py", "Ezek/ezek_residual_gram_families_v6.v1.json", "Ezek/ezek_controlling_agent_rulings_e11.v1.json (ruling S4-GRAMS)", "Ezek/freeze/CYCLE_STATE.md (2026-09-14 #e11 landed)"], "metric": {"sessions": 2, "records_written_by_the_second_session": 0, "families_named_by_S4_11": 2, "families_measured": 3, "rows_per_family": 9, "headroom": 0}, "verdict": "worked", "recommendation": "TREAT A PEER SESSION'S FINDING AS A CANDIDATE AND RE-DERIVE IT BEFORE USING IT - that is what made this safe to act on inside a running execution. Correct a running agent BY MESSAGE, never by editing the record it pinned; SendMessage is available in this runtime, which the resume prompt's OPERATIONAL LAWS paragraph still denies, and that sentence should be corrected in the next prompt. For the overlap itself: OW-11's authority is worded singular, so one writer is the rule; a second session is worth keeping alive as a read-only measurer, which is decorrelation the campaign already values.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0028", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "e11_landed", "element": "control", "observation": "A TRANSCRIPT AUDIT THAT REGEXES WHOLE LINES MEASURES THE FILES THE AGENT READ, NOT THE AGENT. The first pass over #e11's transcript reported 9 E-19 listing idioms. Every one was a false positive: the brief ORDERED the agent to read campaign/_transcript_coverage_census.py (body contains SP.rglob) and Ezek/_coverage_statement_ezek.py (body contains .is_dir()), and those bytes come back inside tool RESULTS. The same pass reported the worktree path absent from a transcript full of worktree reads, because JSON escapes backslashes and the needle did not. Rebuilt over TOOL INPUTS ONLY the audit reads 54 tool calls, 0 listing idioms in any input, 5 writes all in its own private scratch, 0 inside the worktree - and #e11's E-19 self-report is CORROBORATED, the first Ezekiel execution at this depth where it is.", "evidence_refs": ["ezek_primaries/audit_e11_transcript.v2.py (orchestrator scratch)", "transcripts/910cbe15-396b-4a0e-82f6-8aa1e2edf1e4/agent-ad298b7e172edbb3e.jsonl", "learning log L-0022 (S4's real lapse, found by an audit at this depth)"], "metric": {"false_positives_in_v1": 9, "real_findings": 0, "tool_calls": 54, "writes_in_the_worktree": 0, "distinct_paths_read": 29}, "verdict": "mixed", "recommendation": "AN AUDIT MUST SEPARATE WHAT THE AGENT ASKED FOR FROM WHAT CAME BACK. Only a tool INPUT can breach E-19; a tool RESULT carrying the idiom is evidence about a FILE. Fix the audit tool before the next landing, because a 9-false-positive audit is worse than none: it would have put a clean execution in the ledger as a lapse, and the campaign's own controls would then have been calibrated on noise. Note the asymmetry with L-0022: the same class of tool, read correctly, found a REAL undisclosed lapse in S4. The instrument is right; the first implementation was not.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0029", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "pre_primaries_batch", "element": "control", "observation": "A REGRESSION THAT PASSED BECAUSE THE TOOL REFUSED TO RUN. The first v7-stage patch to _cwo_coverage_s2_ezek.py reported all three v6 coverage reports byte-identical and was installed on that evidence. The tool had ABORTED on every run - the regression invoked it with the suite COPY of the rows while the live reports name the CHAIN HEAD - so it wrote nothing and the before/after digests were trivially equal. Caught by reading the tool's stderr rather than only its digests. Re-run correctly against the installed tool it passed for the right reason, and the sibling patch was written to assert exit 0 as well as byte equality.", "evidence_refs": ["campaign/receipts/ezek_tools_install_v7_coverage_stage.json", "ezek_primaries/patch_coverage_s2_v7.py", "ezek_primaries/patch_coverage_e10_v7.py"], "metric": {"reports_compared": 3, "reports_the_tool_actually_wrote": 0, "false_pass": true, "real_pass_after_correction": true}, "verdict": "failed", "recommendation": "A BYTE-EQUALITY REGRESSION MUST ASSERT THE TOOL RAN. Compare digests AND require exit 0; better, require a positive signal that the tool produced the artifact this time. This is the 'a coverage statement is not coverage' family applied to a tool check: an assertion about an unchanged file says nothing unless something was supposed to change it. Add the exit-code assertion to every install-regression template in the playbook.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0030", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "toolfix6", "element": "tool", "observation": "CURING A ROUTE DEFECT SURFACED THREE MORE OF THE SAME SHAPE, ALL IN THE CURE. (1) The census glob **/transcript_manifest.v*.json matched the CANDIDATE manifest the controlling agent had REFUSED and ordered kept only as the record of a defect, so its 17 blank attempt ids reached the retention union - caught because preserved_not_a_receipt_attempt printed ['']. (2) The blank-id refusal was written for the two NEW routes and not for the manifest route it was added beside. (3) The Q8 column relabel was applied to the DICT KEY rather than the presentation, breaking its consumer in the same file. Separately, an agent-id-only lookup reported UNAVAILABLE for 19 executions whose transcripts are demonstrably preserved, because they predate L-0017's write-the-map-at-launch rule; a disclosed fallback to the attempt id took the observed-now column from A 62 to A 78.", "evidence_refs": ["campaign/receipts/ezek_tools_install_toolfix6.json", "campaign/transcript_coverage_census.v2.json", "Ezek/coverage/coverage_statement.pre_primaries.json", "campaign/finding_ezek_transcript_manifest_stale_census_blind.v2.json"], "metric": {"ezek_retained_before": 5, "ezek_retained_after": 63, "attempts": 66, "q8_observed_now_before": "A 3, B 35, unknown 42", "q8_observed_now_after": "A 78, UNAVAILABLE 3", "defects_found_in_the_cure": 3, "books_unchanged": 5}, "verdict": "mixed", "recommendation": "WHEN A FIX ENUMERATES ARTIFACTS BY GLOB, THE SET IT MATCHES IS PART OF THE FIX. A record kept as evidence of a defect must not sit where a consumer's glob will read it as a source; mark it in the NAME and have every consumer skip marked names, because a directory convention alone will not survive the next tool. And a guard added for a new route belongs on every route at once - this is the campaign's own finding_correction_text_carries_its_own_defects rule applied to code rather than prose: treat the cure as new unverified work and sweep it before trusting it.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0031", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "s5_landed", "element": "control", "observation": "THE TRANSCRIPT AUDIT MISSED A LAPSE THE AGENT ITSELF DISCLOSED. S5's E-19 self-report named one deviation: a Grep whose path was a DIRECTORY with a filename filter rather than a file's exact path. The orchestrator's audit reported 0 listing idioms, because it searches tool inputs for SHELL IDIOMS (ls, Test-Path, rglob) and a tool can breach E-19 purely through its PARAMETERS. A structural test was added - a `glob` parameter, or a `path` that resolves to a directory - after which the audit finds exactly that call, and #e11 re-audited under the corrected instrument still reads 0.", "evidence_refs": ["ezek_primaries/audit_e11_transcript.v2.py (orchestrator scratch)", "Ezek/ezek_cwo24_transcript_review_S5.json (e19_selfreport)", "learning log L-0022 (S4's lapse, found BY the audit), L-0028 (the audit's false positives)"], "metric": {"lapses_disclosed_by_the_agent": 1, "lapses_found_by_the_audit_before_the_fix": 0, "found_after_the_fix": 1, "false_positives_introduced": 0}, "verdict": "mixed", "recommendation": "AN AUDIT MUST TEST A TOOL CALL'S SHAPE, NOT ONLY ITS TEXT. Idiom matching catches a shell command and misses a structured tool doing the same thing through its arguments. Test the parameters: any `glob`, any `path` that is a directory, any tool whose name is a listing. Note the sequence across L-0022, L-0028 and this entry: the instrument found a real lapse, then produced nine false ones, then missed a real one. Each time it was corrected by comparing it against a known answer - which is the only way an audit tool earns trust.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0032", "date": "2026-09-14", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "s5_landed", "element": "control", "observation": "THE DISTINCT CHECK PAID FOR ITSELF ON THE ORCHESTRATOR'S OWN TOOLS, WHICH IS WHY OW-13 (c) EXISTS. S5 returned fit_to_accept with nothing routed to the controlling agent, and still produced four findings - all four against the ORCHESTRATOR, none against the corpus. S5-01: the agent-id fallback the orchestrator added to rescue 19 UNAVAILABLE rows cites a SIBLING EXECUTION's transcript for one row, because it matches on agent id without cross-checking execution_id. S5-02: an UNAVAILABLE reason string that misstates a branch. S5-03: the TOOLFIX-6 receipt credits a selftest vector with a case it does not carry. And on the ruling discrepancy it AGREED with the orchestrator's conclusion while showing its stated REASON was wrong - the intersection rule would have read 63 either way, because the wave keys are not receipt attempts. A landing validator's own false positive was found in the same step, from the opposite direction.", "evidence_refs": ["Ezek/ezek_cwo24_transcript_review_S5.json", "Ezek/ezek_rulings_attempt_receipts.jsonl (the S5 amendment)", "campaign/receipts/ezek_tools_install_toolfix6.json"], "metric": {"findings": 4, "against_the_corpus": 0, "against_the_orchestrator": 4, "routed_to_the_controlling_agent": 0, "validator_false_positives_found": 1, "tools_it_reproduced_byte_for_byte": 2, "reports_it_reproduced": 16}, "verdict": "worked", "recommendation": "KEEP THE RULE THAT AN OPUS-AUTHORED TOOL CHANGE UNDER A GATE-BEARING FIGURE IS READ BY A FABLE EXECUTION. Every defect this check found was in work the orchestrator had already checked and believed correct, and none of them moved a number - which is precisely the kind of defect that survives self-review and surfaces later as a false record. Also: give the checker the places where the RULING and the ARTIFACTS disagree and ask it to adjudicate, rather than restating the ruling. It corrected the orchestrator's reasoning on one of them, which a brief that hid the disagreement would have foreclosed.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0033", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "primaries_pilot", "element": "control", "observation": "PILOTING TWO AGENTS BEFORE FORTY-FOUR CAUGHT A DEFECT THAT WOULD HAVE MADE EVERY PACKET UNLANDABLE. The primaries' brief specified items keyed `row`, a `grounds` field and `lane`; the landing validator requires `row_id`, `claim` and `role: primary_LF`. That is not a cosmetic mismatch: with `row` the validator raises TypeError on sorted(item_rows) over a list of None. Proved by running a packet built to the corrected brief through the real validator (0 problems) and one built to the old brief through it (crash). Two further pre-launch defects: the cluster plan claimed the v7 corpus while carrying v6's digest, and the launch messages carried the pre-fix brief digest.", "evidence_refs": ["Ezek/PRIMARY_BRIEF_LF.md", "Ezek/PRIMARY_BRIEF_OL.md", "Ezek/_land_review_packet_ezek.py", "Ezek/review_clusters.json", "Ezek/reviews/rev_LF_c01.json"], "metric": {"pilot_agents": 2, "agents_protected": 42, "pre_launch_defects_caught": 3, "packets_that_would_have_crashed_the_lander": 44}, "verdict": "worked", "recommendation": "PILOT EVERY WIDE WAVE, and make the pilot's pass condition a LANDED packet rather than a returned one. L-0018 says check a brief's schema against its landing validator; this says the check is not enough on its own - build a packet to the brief and run it through the validator, because a name match does not prove a shape match. At 44 executions the pilot costs about 5% of the wave and insures the other 95%.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0034", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "primaries_pilot", "element": "control", "observation": "THE DUAL-BLIND PAIR CONVERGED ON A REAL DEFECT, AND THE OL LANE CITED A QUOTATION THAT IS NOT IN THE WITNESS. Both lanes independently found MT 3:9 to be the fourth, not the third, rebellious-house verse, and both saw it contradict ordinals stated correctly elsewhere in the same cluster - the first evidence that #e11's decorrelation-by-design works with both lanes on one model. Separately, E-01's normalizer over REVIEW PROSE flagged two Hebrew strings in the OL packet; neither occurs in Ezek_oshb.txt. The packet had assembled a ketiv/qere composite, merging fragments of two words and changing U+05A5 MERKHA to U+0594 ZAQEF QATAN. Its conclusion was right and its evidence was fabricated.", "evidence_refs": ["campaign/finding_ol_primary_assembled_a_hebrew_quotation.v1.json", "Ezek/reviews/rev_OL_c01.json", "Ezek/reviews/rev_LF_c01.json", "campaign/finding_correction_text_carries_its_own_defects.v1.json"], "metric": {"lanes": 2, "independent_convergences_on_one_defect": 1, "fabricated_quotations": 1, "conclusion_correct": true, "corpus_impact": 0}, "verdict": "mixed", "recommendation": "RUN E-01 OVER REVIEW PROSE, NOT ONLY OVER ROWS - it is the only check that distinguishes a copied quotation from an assembled one, and a downstream lane reading the packet alone cannot. Put the copy-never-assemble rule in the ORIGINAL-LANGUAGE brief with a self-check the reviewer runs before returning, and require the count checked in verification_evidence. Note the shape: a correct finding resting on invented evidence is more dangerous than a wrong finding, because it survives review on its conclusion.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0035", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "primaries_pilot", "element": "tool", "observation": "THE IN-FLIGHT PIN GUARD READ CLEAR ON A BRIEF TWO RUNNING AGENTS WERE READING. With four executions in flight, two of them OL, `_inflight_pin_guard.py --target Ezek/PRIMARY_BRIEF_OL.md` returned CLEAR and an empty pinned_by, while listing those same executions as in_flight. It keys on whether a running execution's brief TABLE pins the target file, not on whether the target IS the brief that execution is reading. The orchestrator held the edit on the playbook's stricter rule and corrected the running agents by message.", "evidence_refs": ["campaign/_inflight_pin_guard.py", "Ezek/PRIMARY_BRIEF_OL.md", "Ezek/freeze/CYCLE_STATE.md (2026-09-15 pilot entry)"], "metric": {"executions_in_flight": 4, "reading_the_target_brief": 2, "guard_verdict": "CLEAR", "edit_made": false}, "verdict": "failed", "recommendation": "TEACH THE GUARD THAT AN EXECUTION'S OWN BRIEF IS PINNED BY IT. The launch record already names each execution's brief; the guard should treat that path as pinned for the life of the execution, exactly as it treats a file named in the brief's input table. Until then the rule holds only because a human-written playbook line is stricter than the tool that is supposed to enforce it, which is the wrong way round - the same asymmetry L-0031 recorded for the transcript audit.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0036", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "primaries_wave", "element": "tool", "observation": "THE IN-FLIGHT PIN GUARD WAS BLIND TO AN ENTIRE WAVE, NOT JUST TO A BRIEF. With eight primaries running, the guard returned CLEAR on both briefs AND on the v7 corpus row file and the cluster plan - every file those eight agents bind by digest. Root cause: every rule it had keyed on the BRIEF NAMING THE AGENT (execution id, template id, attempt-id fallback), and nothing keyed on the AGENT NAMING THE BRIEF. A brief shared by 22 executions names none of them and does not list itself, so not one line of its input table was reachable. L-0035 recorded the cure as 'the launch record already names each execution's brief': that premise was FALSE - no transcript-map entry had ever carried a brief field, so fixing the guard alone would have changed nothing. The fix is two-part (guard rule + launch record), and the proof that the premise was wrong is that the new guard's output on ten real targets was BYTE-IDENTICAL to the old one's until the field was backfilled.", "evidence_refs": ["campaign/receipts/tool_install_inflight_pin_guard_ownbrief.v1.json", "campaign/finding_pin_guard_failed_open_on_an_entire_wave.v1.json", "campaign/_inflight_pin_guard.py", "Ezek/_transcript_map.ezek.json"], "metric": {"executions_in_flight": 8, "targets_wrongly_CLEAR": 4, "selftest_vectors_before": 10, "selftest_vectors_after": 17, "real_tree_inertness_regression_identical": true, "writes_to_a_pinned_file": 0}, "verdict": "failed", "recommendation": "WHEN A GUARD ASKS 'DOES ANYTHING PIN THIS FILE?', CHECK THAT THE ANSWER CAN EVER BE YES FOR THE WAVE YOU ARE ABOUT TO RUN. The cheap test is to run the guard on a file you KNOW a running execution holds and require PINNED; a guard that has never returned PINNED for a given wave shape has not been tested, only run. And when a learning entry proposes a cure that rests on a record already containing a field, VERIFY the field exists before closing the entry - L-0035 sat for a day describing a one-line fix to a two-part defect.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0037", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "primaries_wave", "element": "tool", "observation": "THE PIN GUARD IS SLOW ENOUGH THAT AN ORCHESTRATOR WOULD BE TEMPTED TO SKIP IT. check() calls pins_for once per (target, in-flight execution) pair, and pins_for re-reads every top-level brief in the book and re-globs the orders files on every call. At eight in flight and ten targets that is eighty full re-reads and the run exceeded a two-minute command timeout. The correctness fix in L-0036 made it no slower, but it did not make it usable at wave scale.", "evidence_refs": ["campaign/_inflight_pin_guard.py", "campaign/receipts/tool_install_inflight_pin_guard_ownbrief.v1.json (limits)"], "metric": {"targets": 10, "in_flight": 8, "full_brief_re_reads": 80, "exceeded_120s_timeout": true}, "verdict": "failed", "recommendation": "BUILD THE TEXT CORPUS ONCE PER (book, run) AND PASS IT DOWN; the per-execution work is then a dictionary lookup. Held back from this change deliberately so that the correctness fix could be proved inert by a byte-identical regression - a performance refactor in the same commit would have destroyed that proof. Route to the controlling agent as a separate change with its own selftest. GENERAL: treat 'this control is slow' as a safety defect and not an inconvenience, because the failure mode is a human or an orchestrator quietly deciding to skip it.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0038", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "primaries_wave", "element": "control", "observation": "THREE LAUNCH BUILDERS TOLD THEIR AGENTS WHEN THE WORKSPACE VALIDATOR RAN, AND NONE OF THEM KNEW. The run time was datetime.now() at message-build time and the lane HEAD was a string literal, while status, mode and the registry digest beside them were read from the real validator output. The stamp happened to be true for #e11 and S5 (validator and build inside one minute) and false by 42 minutes for the 14 landed primaries. It became visible only because a regeneration restamped an unchanged validator run twelve hours later; those messages were rebuilt against a fresh run before any agent saw them.", "evidence_refs": ["campaign/finding_launch_messages_stamped_a_validator_run_time.v1.json", "ezek_primaries/test_val_line_measured.py", "ezek_primaries/_primaries_launch_messages.py", "ezek_primaries/_e11_queue_and_brief.py", "ezek_primaries/_s5_brief.py"], "metric": {"builders_affected": 3, "executions_given_a_false_time": 14, "executions_accidentally_given_a_true_time": 2, "overstatement_minutes": 42, "hardcoded_head_was_correct": true, "substantive_claims_wrong": 0, "regression_vectors": 9}, "verdict": "failed", "recommendation": "A MESSAGE THAT ASSERTS A GOVERNANCE FACT MUST READ EVERY PART OF IT FROM THE EVIDENCE, INCLUDING THE BORING PARTS. The digests in these lines were pinned and checked obsessively while the timestamp and the commit id beside them were invented, because they looked like formatting rather than claims. Two rules worth generalising: where a tool emits no timestamp, use the mtime of its OUTPUT rather than the clock, because that is evidence and a clock reading is not; and say what is actually known - 'validator output written' is checkable, 'run' was not. Note also that the coincidental truth for #e11 and S5 is the dangerous part: a stamped value that is usually right is how a construction defect survives review.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0039", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "primaries_wave", "element": "control", "observation": "THE RULE WRITTEN AFTER THE c01 HEBREW INCIDENT CAUGHT c01'S OWN DEFECT CLASS THREE CLUSTERS LATER, BEFORE RETURN. ezek_pr_OL_c08_a1#e1's first build hand-transcribed its Hebrew as escape sequences and corrupted accents in 16 of 19 runs. The brief's mandated pre-return collation - the clause added because of c01 - caught it; the agent disclosed it unprompted, root-caused it to hand-authored escapes, installed a runtime-slicing cure in its own builder that asserts each slice against the witness and refuses to write otherwise, and re-verified 0 of 19 absent. The corrupted build was never handed on. The agent had no way to know the c01 incident had occurred beyond the brief's own anonymous statement of it, and blindness kept c01's packet out of reach.", "evidence_refs": ["campaign/finding_the_c01_hebrew_rule_caught_its_own_defect_class_in_c08.v1.json", "campaign/finding_ol_primary_assembled_a_hebrew_quotation.v1.json", "Ezek/reviews/rev_OL_c08.json", "Ezek/PRIMARY_BRIEF_OL.md"], "metric": {"runs_corrupted_in_the_first_build": 16, "runs_total": 19, "caught_before_return": true, "clusters_between_rule_and_catch": 3, "runs_absent_after_cure": 0, "form_problems_at_landing": 0}, "verdict": "worked", "recommendation": "KEEP THIS CLAUSE IN EVERY LATER BRIEF AND DO NOT LET IT DECAY INTO BOILERPLATE - it is now the only control in this campaign with direct evidence of having stopped the defect it was written for. GENERALISE THE SHAPE, not the topic: the clause that fired is a MANDATED MECHANICAL RE-CHECK OF THE FINISHED ARTEFACT AGAINST ITS SOURCE, with the count reported in the deliverable. An agent cannot satisfy that by being careful; it has to run it. Every wave that produces quotations, digests or counts should carry the equivalent clause, and the report of the count is what makes the omission visible. AND RECORD SUCCESSES: a ledger of 60-odd failures and no record of which controls ever fired cannot tell a future orchestrator what is safe to simplify.", "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0040", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "primaries_wave", "element": "durability", "observation": "A WORKER WHOSE ONLY WRITE HAPPENS AT THE END LOSES ALL OF ITS WORK, NOT A DELTA. A runtime watchdog killed four of six in-flight primaries mid-verification. Every one had finished most of its review. MEASURED immediately after by exact path: four deliverables ABSENT, six runtime output files 0 bytes, and a positive control on a landed cluster PRESENT - which is what made the absence a finding rather than a wrong path. The four agents were then unreachable, so nothing could be resumed. The root cause is orchestration design, not the runtime: a watchdog kill is a thing to design for.", "recommendation": "Order every long-running worker to WRITE EARLY AND REWRITE: write the deliverable as soon as one finding per unit exists, with outstanding checks named in its own uncertainty field, then finish verifying and rewrite the same path. Say in the brief that a careful worker's instinct is to withhold output until it is clean and that this instinct is exactly what a watchdog converts into total loss.", "verdict": "ADOPTED - carried into BIBLE_CHUNKING_METHOD.v5 section 10 and ledger E-29", "metric": "4 of 6 executions lost, 0 bytes recoverable; after the change, 4 of 4 re-executions and 4 of 4 peers so far wrote an early draft and rewrote it", "evidence_refs": ["ledger E-29", "BIBLE_CHUNKING_METHOD.v5.md section 10"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0041", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "primaries_wave", "element": "durability", "observation": "THE WRITE-EARLY RULE PAID A SECOND DIVIDEND NOBODY COSTED: IT MADE PROGRESS OBSERVABLE. Because each worker now writes a partial deliverable, the orchestrator can measure a running agent's progress by the size and mtime of its own output file, by exact path, without reading a transcript that would overflow context. A stalled agent becomes distinguishable from a slow one.", "recommendation": "Treat the early write as an orchestration instrument and not only as insurance. Check running workers by their partial output rather than by polling them or reading their transcripts.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "four running peers measured mid-flight at 16240 / 7901 / 15579 bytes and one not yet writing, against landed finals of 110000-123000 bytes", "evidence_refs": ["this session's goal check-in"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0042", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "primaries_wave", "element": "control", "observation": "A PROVENANCE CONTROL TURNED OUT TO BE THE ONLY DURABILITY CONTROL IN THE WAVE. OW-18 required each reviewer to write its own final message to a file so the record would not have to be transcribed. That requirement, justified purely on honesty grounds, is the sole reason one of the six killed-or-completed executions survived intact.", "recommendation": "When a control is justified on one ground, do not retire it because that ground looks redundant. Record what each control has actually caught, so the load a control carries is visible before someone simplifies it away.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "1 of 6 executions survived, and only via the OW-18 clause", "evidence_refs": ["ledger E-29", "ledger OW-18"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0043", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "method_record", "element": "verification", "observation": "A COUNT-CHECKED EDIT SCRIPT PROVES WHAT IT DID AND SAYS NOTHING ABOUT WHAT ITS PROSE CLAIMED IT DID. The method record's own version change table listed two changes the document did not contain. Every count check passed, because a count check verifies the edits made and never the edits claimed. It was the file that defines implied provenance as assertion, and the change table is the place a later reader trusts precisely so they need not diff two versions.", "recommendation": "Wherever a document describes its own diff - change tables, release notes, migration notes, commit messages - add a check that extracts each named section from both versions and asserts they differ. Four lines. The cure is the check and not more care.", "verdict": "ADOPTED - the builder now verifies every section its table names", "metric": "2 false rows of 8; after the fix, 5 of 5 claimed sections measurably differ, verdict GREEN", "evidence_refs": ["ledger E-30", "BIBLE_CHUNKING_METHOD.v5.md section 0"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0044", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "peer_round", "element": "validator", "observation": "A ZERO FLAG COUNT IS AN UNDER-REPORT, NOT A CLEAN BILL. That is a peer's phrase and it has held on every group so far. The web_quotes member reported ZERO flags for four consecutive peer groups while each of those peers, applying the same ruling by hand against the bytes, found qualifying runs - one in 13 of its 14 rows, the longest 19 words, one an entire verse. Separately the mark_symmetry member is structurally blind to DIRECTION: it tests whether a mark is disclosed, never which way the row reads it, so the most consequential mark class is invisible to it.", "recommendation": "Never let a member's silence stand as evidence of absence. For every automated member, have at least one reading lane apply the same rule by hand on its own rows and report its own count beside the member's. Where the two diverge, the member's number is a floor.", "verdict": "ADOPTED for this book; a directional-claim arm is routed to the controlling agent for a later book", "metric": "web_quotes: 0 reported vs 34 + 11 + 3 + 4 found by hand on four groups; the orchestrator's own corrected arm then found 98 across 65 of 145 rows", "evidence_refs": ["ezek_controlling_agent_queue_e13.v1.json E13-01", "E13-16"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0045", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "peer_round", "element": "orchestration", "observation": "THE ORCHESTRATOR'S OWN IMPLEMENTATION OF A RULING NEEDED THE PEERS TO CORRECT IT - THREE TIMES ON ONE ARM. Executing the content-quotation ruling produced an arm that keyed on a field's own reference, so the very class the ruling targets - a quotation citing no reference - was invisible. Widening it introduced a STALE ROW REFERENCE, because the row loop closes before the string loop, which would have compared every field against the last row's span. Each defect surfaced because a peer ran the check by hand and reported a count the tool could not produce.", "recommendation": "When a ruling is implemented as a tool, do not trust the tool's census until a reading lane has run the same rule by hand on a sample and the two counts have been compared. Sequence the hand audit BEFORE the tool's output is used to build any worklist.", "verdict": "ADOPTED - the author-wave worklist is ordered to use the union of tool output and peer findings", "metric": "3 successive defects in one arm; hits 15 -> 98 after correction, still below the peers' hand rate", "evidence_refs": ["E13-01"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0046", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "peer_round", "element": "validator", "observation": "A VALIDATOR MEMBER'S GENUINE RATE IS DRIVEN BY WHAT A CLUSTER'S PROSE HAPPENS TO CONTAIN, NOT BY THE MEMBER'S ACCURACY. Three peers measured the universals member at 0 of 50, 15 of 62 and 16 of 51 genuine over their own attributed flags. One peer decomposed its genuine set and showed it clustering around a single refrain list and one rewording class. A corpus-wide rate inferred from the first sample would have been wrong by more than 30 points.", "recommendation": "Never scope a dampener, a suppression or a worklist from one group's flag-answer rate. Require at least three groups before treating a rate as a property of the member, and record each group's rate separately.", "verdict": "ADOPTED - the orchestrator's own one-sample inference was withdrawn on the record rather than edited away", "metric": "0%, 24%, 31% over three groups", "evidence_refs": ["E13-04", "E13-11", "E13-18"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0047", "date": "2026-09-15", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "peer_round", "element": "control", "observation": "A RULE WRITTEN TO STOP FALSE CHALLENGES CAN BAR A VERIFIED ONE, AND A PEER WILL OBEY IT AND SAY SO. Ruling A1 tells a lane not to challenge a properly labelled read form. A peer measured a lane's challenge to be factually TRUE on the bytes - the form it named occurs at zero verses - measured the row's read form to be properly labelled, declined the challenge under A1, and recorded that it had done so and why.", "recommendation": "When writing a rule that bars a class of challenge, state whether it bars a challenge whose FACT is verified, and require the lane to record any finding it suppresses under the rule so the suppression is visible rather than silent.", "verdict": "ROUTED to the controlling agent, undecided", "metric": "1 instance, disclosed by the peer unprompted", "evidence_refs": ["E13-15"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0048", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "author_wave", "element": "brief", "observation": "A BRIEF WRITTEN FROM THE RULINGS, NEVER CHECKED AGAINST THE VALIDATOR SUITE, TOOK A GREEN CORPUS RED. The rulings say what to repair; the suite decides what correct looks like. Three checks went RED, and one duty was not merely omitted but CONTRADICTED: a six-word annotation cap made a required phrase unwritable.", "recommendation": "Before any author launch run tools/check_brief_vs_suite.py, which reads the suite's member list from the suite's own source and fails on any member with neither a duty nor an out-of-scope declaration. Test the brief's PROHIBITIONS against the gate as well as its duties.", "verdict": "ADOPTED - tool built and validated against its own failure case", "metric": "3 checks regressed; 96 register flags from 0", "evidence_refs": ["E13-78", "E13-80", "E-34"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0049", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "reporting", "element": "control", "observation": "AN ORDERED CLAUSE WAS REPORTED APPLIED AND NEVER IMPLEMENTED, and the false claim reached a ruling. The report came from the worker's memory of what it did, not from the order.", "recommendation": "Generate every completion report by iterating the ORDER'S OWN CLAUSE LIST with a measured predicate per clause; an unmeasurable clause is reported UNCLEAR, never passed.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "1 clause, 1 wasted ruling round", "evidence_refs": ["E13-59", "E-31"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0050", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "worklist", "element": "control", "observation": "A WORKLIST BUILT FROM THE CLASSES THE BUILDER THOUGHT OF, NOT THE ORDER'S CLASS LIST, passed a gate that asserted every order had an ITEM - while four ordered items never reached a row and every sweep reported full parity.", "recommendation": "Test DISCHARGE, not existence: an order-to-edit trace string-tests the CURRENT row for each order's verses and device words. Derive the order set from all rulings, never from the artifact you already hold.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "17 rows with an order and no item; 4 undischarged under full parity", "evidence_refs": ["E13-63", "E13-65", "E-32"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0051", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "distinct_check", "element": "lens", "observation": "SET EQUALITY BETWEEN TWO INDEPENDENT IMPLEMENTATIONS OF ONE DEFINITION WAS ONE LENS: both read the same clause's enumeration and missed the same 53 citations. Independent authorship removes correlated bugs, not a correlated premise. An author reading a row's prose caught it.", "recommendation": "A check re-deriving a figure from the SAME specification tests implementation fidelity; only one re-deriving it from the SOURCE tests coverage. Never report the first as the second.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "292/292 set equality, 18% short", "evidence_refs": ["E13-77", "E-33"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0052", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "review_slices", "element": "brief", "observation": "THREE SPOT LANES GIVEN BEFORE/AFTER BYTES BUT NOT THE ORDERS REACHED THREE DIFFERENT RE-DECISION THRESHOLDS; six of seven rows they flagged as re-decided were executing ground changes a ruling had ordered by name.", "recommendation": "Every review and author slice carries, per row, the orders that applied to it as DATA, with the rule that an order is authority for exactly what it names and nothing adjacent.", "verdict": "ADOPTED by ruling (#e15 Q1)", "metric": "6 of 7 flags were ordered changes", "evidence_refs": ["E13-91", "E13-92"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0053", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "in_flight", "element": "control", "observation": "A PINNED INPUT WAS MUTATED WHILE AN AGENT RAN AGAINST IT, and the running agent had to be messaged a new digest.", "recommendation": "An artifact an in-flight execution depends on is FROZEN for the duration. Run SP/campaign/_inflight_pin_guard.py before every record write and SP/campaign/_brief_pin_check.py before every launch - both were skipped at points this day.", "verdict": "ADOPTED - mandatory from Daniel's first act", "metric": "1 containment; 2 launches without the pin check", "evidence_refs": ["E13-81", "E13-99"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0054", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "rulings", "element": "control", "observation": "A RULING'S COUNT CHECK WAS BUILT ON A BASE INHERITED FROM AN EARLIER RULING THAT CONTRADICTED ITS OWN LIST; the later answer coincided with the earlier TRUE count, which hid it through four review rounds. 'Verify by count at apply time' is what caught it.", "recommendation": "Every count check asserts the ordered DELTA against a base re-measured in the same execution; a stated base is a cross-check allowed to disagree, and a disagreement refuses the apply until the chain is explained.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "base 27 stated, 22 measured", "evidence_refs": ["E13-94", "E-35"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0055", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "verification", "element": "control", "observation": "A DISTINCT CHECK REPORTED GREEN WHILE COMPARING NOTHING - shell-heredoc-mangled word boundaries (the third such instance) left every pattern inert - and the step-2 gate built earlier had an inert arm keyed on a field the member never sets. The second was found by an author lane, not by the orchestrator.", "recommendation": "Every check publishes its denominator and a zero denominator is BROKEN; a negative finding runs positive controls drawn from measurements already made; fixtures gate the verdict; recording a class obliges auditing every sibling check the same day. Patterns never go through a shell heredoc.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "111 comparisons reported as 0 disagreements from 0; 1 inert gate arm", "evidence_refs": ["E13-96", "E13-99", "E-36"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0056", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "author_lanes", "element": "lens", "observation": "TWO BLIND LANES ANSWERED THE SAME BROKEN GATE IN OPPOSITE WAYS: one self-censored verse numbers to avoid a failure it expected, the other named thirteen uncovered citations on purpose and reported the gate defect. A single lane would have shipped either a weaker row or an unexplained pass.", "recommendation": "Keep two blind lanes on authoring as well as review (OW-19), and read their DIVERGENCES first at reconciliation - a divergence on a mechanical matter usually points at the instruction or the gate, not at an author.", "verdict": "OBSERVED - reconciliation pending", "metric": "13 uncovered citations vs 0; 1 gate defect found", "evidence_refs": ["E13-98", "E13-99"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0057", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "durability", "element": "control", "observation": "THE ITEMIZED RECORD STAYED CURRENT WHILE THE RESUME CARRIERS STAYED STALE FOR MANY PHASES, because the carriers are written at a clear and the work ran through compactions. The safe-to-clear check refused, five of its own must-pass fixtures with it, and its 'do not print any prompt' rule held.", "recommendation": "Append a CURSOR at every phase transition; run the safe-to-clear check at every landing as a drift detector; hold a compaction to the same durability law as a clear.", "verdict": "ADOPTED - written into the re-versioned resume prompt", "metric": "carriers stale from the c12 primaries cursor to REPAIR-2", "evidence_refs": ["E13-99", "E-37"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0058", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "budget", "element": "control", "observation": "A CENSUS REPORTED '9 ATTEMPTS WITH NO FIGURE' THAT WAS WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS: it counted amendment rows as attempts, ignored a note field where two attempts DO declare UNAVAILABLE, and a first fix dropped an amendment carrying a real 646,412.", "recommendation": "Resolve amendments, never skip them; read every field a record uses to declare unavailability; close a blank by APPENDED amendment with the pre-image verified as a byte prefix.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "0 undeclared blanks, lower bound 39,426,659", "evidence_refs": ["E13-95"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "id": "L-0059", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "stage": "windows_runtime", "element": "tooling", "observation": "THE VALIDATOR SUITE CRASHED ON WINDOWS because piped members wrote the ANSI codepage while the suite decoded UTF-8.", "recommendation": "Launch the suite and any tool that pipes Hebrew with PYTHONUTF8=1 in the environment of the whole process tree.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "1 crash, 0 wrong results", "evidence_refs": ["E13-99"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "id": "L-0060", "stage": "adjudication", "element": "brief_design", "observation": "Two blind lanes split 17 items on a class question (forward-merge rivals): one applied a convention it labelled INFERRED, one stopped. The adjudication brief stated the divergence without a preference and let the adjudicator decide only if the clause as written decides; it routed the class to #e16 with both readings rather than adopting either convention.", "recommendation": "When lanes split on what a rule means rather than on what the text says, give the adjudicator an explicit routing path and forbid inventing the rule.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "17 routed items, 0 invented conventions written", "evidence_refs": ["E13-106"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "id": "L-0061", "stage": "apply", "element": "control", "observation": "Before applying the step-4c proposal the digest of the gate's candidate rows file was compared with the harness's planned post-image; they were identical (2d7160f7).", "recommendation": "Make gate-candidate / harness-plan digest parity a standard pre-apply check: it proves the rows that were checked are the rows that get written.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "1 of 1 parity match", "evidence_refs": ["E13-106"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "id": "L-0062", "stage": "orchestration", "element": "idle_time", "observation": "While an adjudication ran, the next three steps' builders, gate and briefs were written and run in probe mode with the brief-versus-suite check. The probes caught a web_quotes row-mapping miss, a peer membership parse miss, a stale-count pattern with 8 of 10 false hits, five and then one missing duty phrases, and a census-absence design error - all before any launch.", "recommendation": "Build and probe the next step's tooling during every agent wait; a probe build writes only to scratch and cannot be launched from.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "7 defects caught pre-launch, 0 reached a lane", "evidence_refs": ["E13-106", "E-40", "E-38 addendum"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-16", "book": "Ezek", "id": "L-0063", "stage": "checkpoint", "element": "carrier_wording", "observation": "The safe-to-clear checker's repairs_done arm refused 'REPAIR-2 step 4c is LANDED' (a repair word and a completion word within 50 characters); the checkpoint tool restored the prior prompt as designed.", "recommendation": "Carrier wording for REPAIR-2 progress: 'the REPAIR-2 sequence stands at step N, with step X recorded in its receipts'.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "2 refusals this session, 0 stale prompts left", "evidence_refs": ["E-37"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-21", "book": "Ezek", "id": "L-0064", "stage": "launch", "element": "failure_diagnosis", "observation": "Two blind audit lanes were refused at launch (HTTP 429, out of usage credits, claude-fable-5-1) with the five-hour window measured at 95 percent. The window was the first true, measurable thing found, so it was adopted as the cause; the lanes were relaunched after it reset to 6 percent and were refused identically. Four dispatches produced no diagnosis. The fifth was made deliberately minimal - one line, no brief, no file read, every tool forbidden, reply OK - and its identical refusal eliminated brief size, pinned-input cost, context, tool budget and both usage windows in a single dispatch.", "recommendation": "Before retrying a launch whose failure cause is INFERRED, spend the smallest dispatch that DISCRIMINATES between the candidate causes rather than the retry that can only confirm one. A one-line no-tool probe costs less than any retry of the real work and refuses in seconds. Standing rule for this campaign: probe Fable before ordering a Fable lane.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "5 dispatches to reach the diagnosis; 1 would have sufficed", "evidence_refs": ["E-48", "E-49", "ezek_close_audit_attempt_receipts.jsonl"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-21", "book": "Ezek", "id": "L-0065", "stage": "close", "element": "scope_definition", "observation": "An owner decision packet stated that a Fable-gated grade was the only thing between Ezekiel and closure and that every non-Fable item was finished. Each item named was individually verified; the SET was invented from the phase's task list. Reading the tool that actually closed Lamentations showed four outstanding passes - the #e16 application, an OPUS-authored postcheck that was never Fable-blocked at all, the Fable stage-1 transcript audits and the Fable OW-6 final check - plus no assembled corpus and no close tool for the book.", "recommendation": "Measure completeness against the mechanism that enforces it, never against the task list: before any near-completion claim, enumerate the close tool's own assertions and the directory each requires, from its bytes. An owner-facing near-completion claim must cite where its definition of complete came from, so a false premise cannot be smuggled into a decision the owner then makes on it.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "1 of 5 outstanding passes identified before reading the gate", "evidence_refs": ["E-50", "EZEK_CLOSE_GATE_OWNER_DECISION.v2.md"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-21", "book": "Ezek", "id": "L-0066", "stage": "review_launch", "element": "brief_generation", "observation": "Both blind lanes' briefs were emitted from ONE retained generator with the pin digests computed from disk at generation time. The two 12,139-byte briefs differ on exactly four lines - the lane letter twice and the lane's own two output paths - proved by a line-level diff recorded as evidence rather than asserted, and the pin table cannot go stale because a stale table cannot pass the pin check it is generated against.", "recommendation": "Treat blindness as a BYTE property, not an intention: generate every lane of a blind set from one text, then record the line-level diff as the evidence that the lanes hold the same rules. A rule that differs by a word between lanes turns a disagreement into an artefact of the briefs.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "4 differing lines; pins MATCH 21 of 21, zero drift, both briefs", "evidence_refs": ["ezek_close_audit_prelaunch.v1.json", "gen_close_audit_brief.py"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-21", "book": "Ezek", "id": "L-0067", "stage": "prelaunch", "element": "control_applicability", "observation": "The checkpoint's own next_job said to run check_brief_vs_suite.py to PASS. It FAILED, because it is an AUTHOR-brief control that demands a duty for each of the 11 row-validator suite members, and an audit lane writes no rows: 1 member out of scope, 10 duties missing. Reporting the FAIL as a PASS would have been a half-truth; skipping it silently would have been another.", "recommendation": "Record an inapplicable control as a THIRD outcome - not a pass, not a skip - with the verdict it actually returned, the reason it does not apply, and what would make it apply. An inapplicable control is evidence about the artifact's kind, and a checklist inherited from a previous phase can name the wrong control for the current artifact.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "1 control recorded as check_NOT_applicable with its FAIL verdict retained", "evidence_refs": ["ezek_close_audit_prelaunch.v1.json", "OW-18"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-21", "book": "Ezek", "id": "L-0068", "stage": "close", "element": "governance_floor_dependency", "observation": "The Fable dependency is COMPILED INTO the close tool, which asserts the model name and the fit_to_close verdict (sp_durable/Lam/_close_book.py lines 58, 60, 61, 88). When the model became unreachable the pipeline therefore REFUSED TO CLOSE rather than closing at a lower standard. No silent downgrade was possible, and the orchestrator - the party the floor exists to check - could not grant itself an exception without visibly rewriting an assertion whose stated purpose is to prevent exactly that.", "recommendation": "Keep the failure mode: a floor should refuse rather than degrade, and the assertion belongs in the tool, not in the prompt. What it additionally needs is a fallback grader DECLARED IN ADVANCE by the owner, with its downgrade stated in the completion receipt's own bytes - so the substitution is a recorded owner decision rather than an improvisation at the gate.", "verdict": "PROPOSED - the fallback grader is an owner decision, not adopted here", "metric": "4 code assertions held; 0 gates weakened", "evidence_refs": ["E-48", "E-49", "OW-6", "OW-13", "EZEK_CLOSE_GATE_OWNER_DECISION.v2.md"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-21", "book": "Ezek", "id": "L-0069", "stage": "bookkeeping", "element": "metric_classifier_coverage", "observation": "The OW-14 metrics refresh DID ingest the five refused Fable dispatches - source sp_durable/Ezek/ezek_close_audit_attempt_receipts.jsonl at sha 5e2e8e51, 5 receipts, and the by_model_ordered histogram for claude-fable-5-1 shows 'FAILED AT LAUNCH': 5. But the adjacent summary counter refused_no_deliverable reads 0 for that model, because the counter keys on a different outcome string than the one launch refusals are receipted with. Both numbers sit in the same file: a reader of the summary sees zero refusals, a reader of the histogram sees five.", "recommendation": "Widen the refused_no_deliverable classifier in sp_durable/campaign/_orchestration_metrics.py to include launch-time refusals, and bump the metrics schema when doing so, because reclassifying changes every historical number the file reports. NOT done here: the file was refreshed minutes earlier, and silently changing a classifier between two refreshes of the same schema version would make two versions disagree with no recorded reason. Generated files are not hand-edited; the generator is authoritative.", "verdict": "PROPOSED - defer to the book-close metrics version bump", "metric": "1 summary counter undercounts 5 receipted launch refusals; histogram is correct", "evidence_refs": ["orchestration_metrics.v1.json@1337016a4b5dc7d790404b640a7fae8e5cde59672c82f44307923f0049bb6416", "ezek_close_audit_attempt_receipts.jsonl@5e2e8e51", "E-49", "OW-18"], "supersedes": null}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-23", "book": "Ezek", "supersedes": null, "id": "L-0070", "stage": "s5_landed", "element": "token_measurement_unit", "observation": "The completion notification's subagent_tokens is the LAST request's context size, not spend (MEASURED on both OW-26 lanes: 101,506 vs 101,293 last-request context; 148,182 equal). Spend as input + cache creation + output, deduplicated by message id, was 576,419 (B) and 855,552 (A).", "recommendation": "At every landing, measure spend from the lane's usage fields (content NOT read) and record it as an m8_attempt_receipt_amendment.v1 row; teach the OW-15 census the unit and re-measure earlier executions where transcripts survive, stating the rest as a residual.", "verdict": "ADOPTED for the merged lanes; census re-measure OWED", "metric": "2 of 2 lanes undercounted by the notification figure (~475K and ~707K); census LOWER_BOUND 70,938,658", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/ezek_merged_close_attempt_receipts.jsonl@18159d16509259c9b8f01045ac40ad5686c4e3ba97c9edd8b0d0edbcb71c887c", "sp_durable/campaign/finding_notification_tokens_are_final_context.v1.json@cf48500d70d45382bc5872184972fa0f7cff1a9934f6e85a3f5858262f31fb23", "ERROR_PATTERN_LEDGER.v1.md@beeb195a1741c7d4ebafe0c321837e98425ae9aa551e4cac522c6ac70db6d07b (E-53, E-54)"]}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-23", "book": "Ezek", "supersedes": null, "id": "L-0071", "stage": "resume", "element": "resume_after_api_429", "observation": "Lane A #e1 stopped on an API 429 after writing 82 work files; it was resumed via SendMessage with the same brief, execution id and output directory, and landed. It was first misjudged as having done no work from its last message.", "recommendation": "Resume the same execution rather than relaunch; write NO receipt row for it until it lands (any amending row reads to the pin guard as a landing); judge an interrupted run by its output directory and usage fields, never its last text; record the stop in the completion row's interruptions field.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "1 resume, 0 relaunches, 0 pins released early (guard in_flight named the lane until landing)", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/ezek_merged_close_attempt_receipts.jsonl@18159d16509259c9b8f01045ac40ad5686c4e3ba97c9edd8b0d0edbcb71c887c", "sp_durable/Ezek/merged_close/landing_manifest.v1.json@642e804db18c4492cae926dda14a5928af6c862af2aad9d492070ffdcd7c46d8", "ERROR_PATTERN_LEDGER.v1.md@beeb195a1741c7d4ebafe0c321837e98425ae9aa551e4cac522c6ac70db6d07b (E-53, E-54)"]}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-23", "book": "Ezek", "supersedes": null, "id": "L-0072", "stage": "adjudication", "element": "verdict_reconciliation_premise_checks", "observation": "Both lanes returned not_fit on different blockers (B: P06-015; A: P03-001, graded LOW by B). A's item-23 not_fit rests on a count whose only hit is a schema example string (MEASURED). Both lanes mark Lam line L60 (final checker == claude-fable-5-1) unmet, which the close gate would refuse even after a clean re-check under OW-25.", "recommendation": "The orchestrator measures a lane's factual premise but never grades; a severity split goes to a named non-orchestrator adjudicator. Before any re-check, make the Ezekiel close tool or brief recognise the OW-25 grader fallback for L60, leaving the Lam tool untouched.", "verdict": "PROPOSED - owner ruling pending at the OW-22 line", "metric": "2 lanes, 2 disjoint blockers, 1 severity split, 1 false-positive premise, 1 latent gate", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/ezek_merged_close_attempt_receipts.jsonl@18159d16509259c9b8f01045ac40ad5686c4e3ba97c9edd8b0d0edbcb71c887c", "sp_durable/Ezek/merged_close/landing_manifest.v1.json@642e804db18c4492cae926dda14a5928af6c862af2aad9d492070ffdcd7c46d8"]}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-23", "book": "Ezek", "supersedes": null, "id": "L-0073", "stage": "census_remeasure", "element": "ceiling_unit", "observation": "Census v4 re-measured 226 attempts from usage fields: 70,938,658 in the notification unit (last-request context), 118,001,439 in the spend unit (input + cache creation + output), 31,601,439 over the OW-27 ceiling of 86,400,000. 42,943,649 of the 47,062,781 gap sits in runs that never compacted (repeated cache writes plus output), not in compaction as E-54 inferred.", "recommendation": "A ceiling names its unit. Record both units at every landing, forecast in the ceiling's unit, and when the units disagree on fit, bring the unit question to the owner instead of taking the favourable reading. Condition a launch on a re-measure, never on a stated headroom.", "verdict": "ADOPTED for the re-measure; the ceiling's unit is PENDING the owner's ruling", "metric": "1 re-measure, 0 lanes dispatched; spend/recorded ratio 1.1-2x in 137 of 185 groups", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/ezek_ow15_position.v4.json@d19134c9926383154103a4360a91a8401357057b1f64ef0d771d9706841696a5", "sp_durable/campaign/finding_census_gap_is_cache_rewrites.v1.json@dd80c3072bb907c260a6e4cc1c8b6ec0d799fa713ec5574b68372beebcec2500", "ERROR_PATTERN_LEDGER.v1.md@6ff62f3381783e8f0aad2ffd9949d420603d51aa937515d3b0e114299c873841 (E-55, OW-27)"]}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-23", "book": "Ezek", "supersedes": null, "id": "L-0074", "stage": "fable_ruling", "element": "fable_call_cost", "observation": "The one owner-authorized Fable call (OW-30) ruled the eight atlas holds in a single pass and returned a standing precedent (P1 release a hold whose row defect is measured absent; P2 a class question releases, or drops from the feed a row graded above medium_low; P3 a defect still present keeps the hold, re-versions the corpus, and needs two blind lanes to re-check it). It measured 65,795 tokens in the notification unit, against an orchestrator estimate of 15-25K (E-58). The brief was small; most of the cost was the call's own context and tool loading.", "recommendation": "Record every Fable ruling's precedent where later books read it, so the same ruling is not bought twice (done: memory fable-hold-precedent-ow30). Estimate a Fable call from measured Fable calls, not from the size of the brief. Propose to the owner a lean Fable agent definition with no tools, a pinned facts file and a fixed output schema.", "verdict": "ADOPTED for the precedent record; PROPOSED for the lean Fable agent definition (owner's decision)", "metric": "1 Fable call, 65,795 notification tokens MEASURED vs 15-25K INFERRED estimate; 8 holds ruled", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/fable_end_review/atlas_hold_ruling.v1.json@24f6f8b0f4b2c4527db95505c9cf44d7a547c68b725675d32aa437d8bc130e5c", "ERROR_PATTERN_LEDGER.v1.md@7d5dc230154568153b11c41d4d3d688f4420b8af5783e038a5c51dc4d94cc5b3 (E-58, OW-30)"]}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-23", "book": "Ezek", "supersedes": null, "id": "L-0075", "stage": "v10_delta_lanes", "element": "lane_cost_vs_estimate", "observation": "The two blind v10 delta lanes both returned fit_to_assemble and fit_to_close. Both judged all eight ruled ids implemented as ruled, and neither found a high or medium residual. They measured 154,052 and 143,375 tokens in the notification unit (spend 195,173 and 157,614), against an INFERRED estimate of 110-130K each taken from the v9 lanes' 134.9K and 134.8K. Lane A exceeded the top of the range by 24K. Lane A's own report gave 28 tool calls; its notification counted 27. The lane disclosed the discrepancy itself.", "recommendation": "Forecast a re-check lane from the largest measured sibling lane plus a growth allowance, never below the last measured pair. Take usage and tool counts from the runtime's usage fields at landing only. A lane's self-report is a claim to check, not a measure.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "2 lanes, 297,427 notification tokens MEASURED vs 220-260K INFERRED; 1 self-report off by 1 tool call", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/merged_close/delta_v10/landing_manifest.v1.json@1aac154d020ecd44c383014c5cdb922dd228baee3cfecc37206b905ba56b544b", "sp_durable/Ezek/repair2/fixround_v10/ezek_fixround_v10_attempt_receipts.jsonl@a285cf717929cf2b2aaab944775c7cd79ecf02c502ded011cd8f68d886f747b4"]}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-23", "book": "Ezek", "supersedes": null, "id": "L-0076", "stage": "v10_build", "element": "text_edit_mechanics", "observation": "Three text-mechanics faults came up while building v10. (1) An anchor written with LF missed a CRLF file; the counted-anchor guard refused, the edit rolled back cleanly, and newline-aware anchors fixed it. (2) An escape-bearing Python snippet sent through a shell heredoc was mangled again, a repeat of an earlier lesson. (3) Appending to an empty receipts file would have started with a stray newline, because the separator test read only 'file does not end in a newline'. The derived landing tool now also tests for an empty file.", "recommendation": "Make anchors newline-aware wherever a file's line endings are not pinned. Write Python that carries regexes or escapes with the file tools, never through a shell heredoc. Any append helper treats an empty file as needing no separator.", "verdict": "ADOPTED", "metric": "3 faults, 0 bad bytes landed (1 refused and rolled back, 1 caught at a rerun, 1 fixed at derivation)", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/repair2/fixround_v10/derive_lane_tools_v10.py@d6ff85ee3f99420fe5f39608a7392ba2387056b2a9d2ca3fd93f742b86711014"]}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-23", "book": "Ezek", "supersedes": null, "id": "L-0077", "stage": "close", "element": "ow29_close_sequence", "observation": "The first dry run showed 122 gates with 0 unmet. The gate test was then updated (53 cases PASS), which changed bytes under Ezek/, so that dry preview's retention and receipt digests went stale (retention 40fd0561 -> f4cda365, receipt e85ccabc -> 8110b4ff). A second dry run with the same arguments showed 0 unmet. --close --acf item22 then wrote a receipt byte-equal to the second preview (8110b4ff). After the close, the coverage validator passed 17 closed books and failed 9 (Eccl, Ezek, Isa, Jer, Job, Lam, Prov, Ps, Song), each on a missing reviews/<book>/review_packets.jsonl (E-57). The failure is on file absence, so the per-book rule is never reached for those books.", "recommendation": "Re-run the dry close immediately before --close whenever anything under the book's folder changed after the last dry run: a dry preview binds only the bytes it saw. Run the coverage validator at every close, and give the owner the review_packets decision: backfill the nine books, or amend the validator's contract for the later mesh.", "verdict": "ADOPTED for the re-dry rule; the review_packets gap is PENDING the owner's decision", "metric": "dry 122/0 twice, close CLOSED (138 chunks, 26/66); coverage validator 17 OK / 9 missing review_packets", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Ezek/_close_book.py@b569755697c98e54a2965c85015de5da27abd5f587e6a0341ba51bfca357831c", "sp_durable/Ezek/_test_close_book_gates.py@d22f7052cae0b504c450c3dccb03f0365bf1fdb7ba3a2d510d50c5955a7f18f6", "receipts/Ezek_completion.json@8110b4ff993940d5d3b3b0ec4ab14b46335f3749fbd8a203cb80479b92c324b6", "ERROR_PATTERN_LEDGER.v1.md@7d5dc230154568153b11c41d4d3d688f4420b8af5783e038a5c51dc4d94cc5b3 (E-57)"]}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-28", "book": "Dan", "supersedes": null, "id": "L-0078", "stage": "phase0_strategy_check", "element": "lane", "observation": "Two blind check lanes (C1, C2; both claude-opus-5-5 under OW-25) checked the strategy candidate .a1. The orchestrator withheld one finding it had measured (md line 23: the literal 'I, Daniel' is in 5 WEB verses; the 9 the md counts are the first-person self-naming family). C1 caught it blind as D6; C2's macro_shape PASS cited the first person in chapters 7-12 and did not question the count. Both returned not_fit: C1 10 defects (9 exact corrections, 1 judgement), C2 8 (5 exact, 3 judgement). Applied in memory to the .a1 files, C1's 9 then C2's 5 exact corrections each find a unique span at their turn and the plan still parses, so no two exact corrections edit the same span. Overlap between the lanes' judgement items was not measured.", "recommendation": "Keep two blind check lanes as the floor (OW-19): on this candidate a single C2 lane would have shipped the miscount. Keep a withheld, orchestrator-measured finding in every strategy check as a sensitivity probe and record CAUGHT or MISSED per lane. Before reconciling, apply both lanes' exact corrections jointly in memory to prove they do not collide.", "verdict": "worked", "metric": "reserved finding: C1 CAUGHT, C2 MISSED; defects C1 10 (9/1), C2 8 (5/3); joint exact corrections 14/14 unique, plan parses; notification-unit tokens C1 135114, C2 104426", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Dan/dan_phase0_attempt_receipts.jsonl@59ac6f99a44e3fdff58eab84345a9121c4d4fa51e0cc237dd654c1111800511e rows 23 and 26"]}
{"schema": "m8_orchestration_learning.v1", "date": "2026-09-28", "book": "Dan", "supersedes": null, "id": "L-0079", "stage": "phase0", "element": "control", "observation": "Daniel Phase 0 has 13 landed executions across 6 jobs. 6 completed. 7 ended FAILED on a runtime limit, not on the work: 4 on the API session limit, 2 on the API weekly limit and 1 on the runtime watchdog. Each failed execution was landed FAILED before its job's next execution was launched, and every relaunch was a fresh execution under a new #eN id.", "recommendation": "Keep the rule: land a limit-stopped lane FAILED, report it to the owner, then relaunch fresh under a new execution id. Expect limit stops at this rate when several lanes run at once, and plan lane concurrency against the remaining session and weekly allowance, not the token ceiling.", "verdict": "mixed", "metric": "13 executions: 6 COMPLETED, 7 FAILED (session limit 4, weekly limit 2, runtime watchdog 1)", "evidence_refs": ["sp_durable/Dan/dan_phase0_attempt_receipts.jsonl@59ac6f99a44e3fdff58eab84345a9121c4d4fa51e0cc237dd654c1111800511e"]}
